WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:05.040
Fans of the Wachowski's sci-fi classic have&nbsp;
known for a while that "The Matrix" franchise is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:10.320
getting a fourth installment. What's more, Lana&nbsp;
Wachowski is set to direct, and Keanu Reeves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:15.280
Carrie-Anne Moss, and a slew of other stars of the&nbsp;
original movies will make their glorious return in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:20.160
"The Matrix 4." The movie will also feature&nbsp;
some brand new additions to the cast, from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:25.840
Jessica Henwick and Neil Patrick Harris to Yahya&nbsp;
Abdul-Mateen II. Oh, and if you're expecting new,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:30.080
cutting-edge camera tricks, such as the original&nbsp;
movie's groundbreaking "bullet time," don’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:34.720
worry — Henwick has already teased there will be&nbsp;
plenty to be excited about on that front, as well.

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:38.320
Warner Bros. just dropped a&nbsp;
heaping helping of "Matrix 4" data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:41.760
at CinemaCon, proving once and for&nbsp;
all that the movie isn't just some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:45.440
plot by a bunch of machines that use&nbsp;
your hopes and dreams as a battery.

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:50.960
With the exception of the 1999 original,&nbsp;
the 2003 animated "The Animatrix," and a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:55.280
bunch of video games, many installments in&nbsp;
"The Matrix" franchise have a penchant for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.280 --> 00:01:00.800
alliterative titles. The second and third movies&nbsp;
in the series, both of which premiered in 2003,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:06.000
were called "The Matrix Reloaded" and "The&nbsp;
Matrix Revolutions," and even the 2001&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:10.720
documentary about the making of the original&nbsp;
movie is known as "The Matrix Revisited."

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:14.560
Warner Bros. has now revealed that "The&nbsp;
Matrix 4" will follow the tradition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:19.440
and its official title is "The Matrix&nbsp;
Resurrections." Speculations about this title&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:24.160
have actually been going on for quite a while,&nbsp;
to the point that Slashfilm jokingly predicted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:29.840
it way back in 2009. Besides giving huge credit&nbsp;
to Slashfilm for nailing that one, the name is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:34.480
pretty fitting considering that it's essentially&nbsp;
resurrecting "The Matrix" franchise. There's also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:38.720
the fact that Keanu Reeves and Carrie-Anne&nbsp;
Moss are set to return in "Resurrections,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.720 --> 00:01:43.040
and their characters died pretty conclusively&nbsp;
in "The Matrix Revolutions." This means that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:47.360
in one way or another, the characters will find&nbsp;
their way back from beyond the veil, hopefully&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:52.160
in a way that allows them to re-enter the fray&nbsp;
while already wearing their iconic sunglasses.

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:57.120
The title reveal, incidentally, also falls in line&nbsp;
with the screening "leak" that dropped in June.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.120 --> 00:02:01.600
The anonymous Twitter account behind the leak&nbsp;
also stated that "The Matrix Resurrections"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:06.720
is thematically close to "The Matrix Reloaded."&nbsp;
It remains to be seen whether this is the case.

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:10.960
The title reveal of "The Matrix Resurrections"&nbsp;
would be exciting news in its own right,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:15.520
but as The Hollywood Reporter notes, Warner&nbsp;
Bros. also used their CinemaCon platform&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:19.120
to screen the first trailer for the&nbsp;
movie. As you'd expect, the trailer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:24.400
which will presumably drop online before long, is&nbsp;
full of massively exciting, mind-bending stuff.

00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:29.520
Reportedly, the trailer shows Reeves' character&nbsp;
back in his everyman Thomas Anderson mode,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:34.000
suppressing his nagging feeling that the world's&nbsp;
slightly off with a steady diet of blue pills&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:39.280
and sessions with his therapist. There's a curious&nbsp;
meeting with Moss' apparently equally amnesic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:44.160
character, and an encounter with Abdul-Mateen's&nbsp;
suspiciously Morpheus-like character.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:47.840
Cue red pill, martial arts training,&nbsp;
and amazing Neo powers — which,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:51.520
apparently, allow Keanu to control&nbsp;
missiles with his mind this time.

00:02:51.520 --> 00:02:56.480
If much of this seems very reminiscent of the&nbsp;
original "The Matrix," it's likely intentional,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.480 --> 00:02:59.440
because apart from the usual&nbsp;
"What is the Matrix?" shenanigans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:04.400
there's clearly another layer of existential&nbsp;
terror lying underneath the surface. Supposedly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:07.520
there's a mirror shot that shows an&nbsp;
older and younger Thomas-slash-Neo,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:12.240
and the fact that pretty much everyone in the&nbsp;
world is terribly interested in their phones&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:16.560
might mean something awful, as well. Once this&nbsp;
trailer officially drops, we’ll be back with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:20.960
video breaking it all down with the easter&nbsp;
eggs, and small details you know and love.

00:03:20.960 --> 00:03:25.200
"The Matrix Resurrections" is currently&nbsp;
slated to premiere on December 22.

